1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:05,365
picachu helps borrowers on Solana decide how much to
repay and how much cash to keep.

2
00:00:05,507 --> 00:00:09,891
Use a limited budget to reduce risk while preserving
funds for other needs.

3
00:00:10,021 --> 00:00:12,402
This walkthrough covers the complete planner,

4
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:14,949
allocation when funds are insufficient,

5
00:00:15,114 --> 00:00:18,085
and a verified repayment receipt on the test network.

6
00:00:18,734 --> 00:00:24,263
First, select the loans to include. This labeled
synthetic example has debts of sixty-five,

7
00:00:24,382 --> 00:00:30,181
fifty-five, and forty-five USDC. Each has one SOL of
collateral at an

8
00:00:30,181 --> 00:00:35,609
assumed price of one hundred dollars. Connecting a
wallet loads supported Kamino Devnet

9
00:00:35,609 --> 00:00:38,442
positions. The wallet balance is shared,

10
00:00:38,583 --> 00:00:41,312
rather than counted once for every position.

11
00:00:41,888 --> 00:00:45,253
Next, the example wallet holds eighty USDC.

12
00:00:45,557 --> 00:00:51,213
Keep twenty, repay at most thirty, and test a thirty
percent fall in SOL's price.

13
00:00:51,355 --> 00:00:56,155
The target is an additional five percent price buffer
after that fall, before liquidation.

14
00:00:56,286 --> 00:01:00,010
The budget is a limit, not a requirement to spend
everything.

15
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:03,216
This is a chosen scenario, not a market forecast.

16
00:01:03,821 --> 00:01:08,193
Loan A needs eleven point eight USDC, loan B one point
eight,

17
00:01:08,439 --> 00:01:13,806
and loan C no extra repayment. The total, thirteen point
six, is below the budget,

18
00:01:13,937 --> 00:01:16,225
leaving sixty-six point four in the wallet.

19
00:01:16,367 --> 00:01:19,316
Each row shows its repayment and scenario buffer.

20
00:01:19,469 --> 00:01:23,505
The core uses the example's liquidation threshold and
borrowing factor;

21
00:01:23,763 --> 00:01:25,820
AI does not invent these amounts.

22
00:01:26,433 --> 00:01:30,411
You can write: repay up to ten USDC, keep twenty,

23
00:01:30,599 --> 00:01:36,661
if SOL falls thirty percent. picachu creates a draft for
review and explicit application.

24
00:01:36,803 --> 00:01:40,480
AI can extract the goal, while the core validates its
values.

25
00:01:40,622 --> 00:01:44,960
If fixed rules are used instead, the interface
identifies that source.

26
00:01:45,102 --> 00:01:48,548
AI cannot decide the allocation or sign a transaction.

27
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:53,505
Ten dollars is three point six USDC short of meeting
every goal.

28
00:01:53,646 --> 00:01:57,532
The warning remains: partial payment is not full
protection.

29
00:01:57,663 --> 00:02:03,736
Explore allocation to compare repayments that reduce
estimated liquidation costs in this scenario.

30
00:02:03,878 --> 00:02:06,456
You can still increase the budget or change the goal.

31
00:02:07,066 --> 00:02:10,338
The model proposes about nine USDC for A,

32
00:02:10,527 --> 00:02:14,586
leaving nearly one dollar unused. The exact amount is
visible.

33
00:02:14,716 --> 00:02:19,158
A still lacks the five percent target buffer, so this
remains partial improvement.

34
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:24,621
The explanation separates loss from one assumed
liquidation event and repayment fees.

35
00:02:24,751 --> 00:02:28,602
Compare no repayment, equal split, risk-first repayment,

36
00:02:28,779 --> 00:02:31,253
and the proposal using the same data and budget.

37
00:02:31,395 --> 00:02:35,605
The lowest cost among these candidates is different from
meeting every goal.

38
00:02:35,723 --> 00:02:38,649
Repayment principal is not liquidation loss.

39
00:02:39,233 --> 00:02:43,534
Review the amount, fee, and expiry, then sign with your
wallet.

40
00:02:43,664 --> 00:02:49,529
Plan changes require another review. Each step opens
only after the previous receipt is verified.

41
00:02:49,648 --> 00:02:52,747
The journal preserves progress across reloads.

42
00:02:52,842 --> 00:02:56,786
An uncertain status blocks further sending until
checked,

43
00:02:56,905 --> 00:03:00,559
avoiding blind retries. Your wallet keeps the private
key.

44
00:03:00,690 --> 00:03:05,050
This is a workflow explanation, not footage of a Phantom
confirmation popup.

45
00:03:05,666 --> 00:03:10,559
This real receipt comes from a separate Vercel API test
with a dedicated Devnet wallet.

46
00:03:10,701 --> 00:03:13,904
The budget and reserve were each one USDC,

47
00:03:14,034 --> 00:03:16,519
with a forty-five percent SOL scenario.

48
00:03:16,627 --> 00:03:21,311
After reviewing the new preview, the wallet repaid about
zero point seven eight;

49
00:03:21,349 --> 00:03:24,795
the full amount is shown. The journal verified the step.

50
00:03:24,925 --> 00:03:28,684
About sixteen point six two remained, above the reserve.

51
00:03:28,802 --> 00:03:32,422
A successful receipt does not mean every current goal is
met;

52
00:03:32,576 --> 00:03:34,621
prices and interest can change.

53
00:03:35,233 --> 00:03:40,160
The model matched twenty-five cases against the Kamino
Devnet executable.

54
00:03:40,279 --> 00:03:44,142
Unsupported versions or configurations disable
allocation.

55
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:47,706
It finds the best result in a finite grid for one event,

56
00:03:47,848 --> 00:03:51,653
without predicting probabilities or cascading
liquidations.

57
00:03:51,795 --> 00:03:54,975
picachu supports one wallet and up to three SOL

58
00:03:55,094 --> 00:03:58,991
and USDC loans. Try the planner, choose your goal,

59
00:03:59,156 --> 00:04:01,838
and inspect the source and evidence on GitHub.
